POST VS PUT in REST interview question asked?
How PUT is considered by Spring or REST, so that it know that it has to perform update operation.
Ans:
POST creates a resource and PUT updates a resource.
You should explain HTTP semantics, idempotency, partial vs. full updates, REST API design, Spring Boot implementation, 
  and real-world scenarios.
POST is generally used to create a resource or ask the server to process a request. 
PUT is generally used to create or replace a resource at a known URI.

Feature	        POST	                              PUT
Primary purpose	Create or process a resource	      Create or replace a resource at a known URI
Resource        URI	Often selected by server	      Selected by client
Idempotent?	    Not inherently	                    Yes, by HTTP semantics
Repeating the 
same request	  May create duplicate resources 
                or repeat processing	              Should have the same intended effect on resource state
Full vs. 
partial update	Can perform either, 
                depending on API contract	          Generally represents replacement, not a partial update
Typical 
response	      201 Created, 200 OK, 
                or 202 Accepted	                    200 OK,204 No Content, or 201 Created if created
Typical 
example	        POST /employees	                    PUT /employees/101


200 OK
201 Created
202 Accepted
204 No Content

PUT - Idempotent
DELETE - Idempotent
GET - Idempotent
POST - Not Idempotent
PATCH - Not Idempotent

Idempotency means that making the same request multiple times has the same intended effect on the server's resource state as 

PUT /api/employees/101
{
  "name": "Prakash",
  "department": "Platform Engineering",
  "salary": 1500000
}

PATCH /api/employees/101
Content-Type: application/merge-patch+json
{
  "department": "Platform Engineering"
}





